WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:05.530
Movies should take all forms, from bullet-filled
action epics to quiet costume dramas.

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:09.960
But sometimes, filmmakers go too far testing
the patience of their audience.

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:13.769
There are some flicks you can't possibly get
through without drooping eyelids.

00:00:13.769 --> 00:00:19.360
So, to save precious hours of your life, here
are some movies that are literally too boring

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:20.360
to watch.

00:00:20.360 --> 00:00:21.380
Last Days

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:25.619
A film loosely based on the last days of Kurt
Cobain's tragic life?

00:00:25.619 --> 00:00:26.619
Sounds like a winner!

00:00:26.619 --> 00:00:31.480
Too bad director Gus Van Sant decided to have
Michael Pitt, playing a Cobain-like musician

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:36.140
named "Blake," just wander around and mumble
incoherently from time to time.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:41.940
In the most thrilling moment from 2005's Last
Days, the camera moves from a drugged-up Blake

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:46.390
to a TV screen on the other side of the living
room, where the video for Boyz II Men's "On

00:00:46.390 --> 00:00:47.740
Bended Knee" is playing.

00:00:47.740 --> 00:00:49.890
It stays there for almost the entire video.

00:00:49.890 --> 00:00:52.570
The camera then moves back to the strung-out
singer.

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:55.829
That's the most action-packed scene in the
entire film.

00:00:55.829 --> 00:00:59.670
When it comes to mumbling musicians, there's
only one for us anyhow.

00:00:59.670 --> 00:01:05.189
"Someone has been in my room and taking my
beers away from my room."

00:01:05.189 --> 00:01:06.920
"I don't think so, darling."

00:01:06.920 --> 00:01:07.920
"Ughhhhh..."

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:10.100
Meet Joe Black

00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:14.770
You wouldn't think a movie about death taking
human form would be a snooze, but this one

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:15.770
is.

00:01:15.770 --> 00:01:21.349
In 1998's Meet Joe Black, Anthony Hopkins
plays Bill Parrish, a billionaire mogul about

00:01:21.349 --> 00:01:23.679
to celebrate his 65th birthday.

00:01:23.679 --> 00:01:28.229
His daughter, played by Claire Forlani, is
considering marrying a guy on the board of

00:01:28.229 --> 00:01:32.899
his company, but Hopkins gives a big speech
about how she must wait for passionate love.

00:01:32.899 --> 00:01:37.310
Later, Forlani meets Brad Pitt in a coffee
shop and, though her heart's all aflutter,

00:01:37.310 --> 00:01:38.899
she doesn't even get his name.

00:01:38.900 --> 00:01:40.300
Then, this happens:

00:01:42.249 --> 00:01:47.140
So, Pitt's dead, and Death takes over his
body.

00:01:47.149 --> 00:01:51.369
Now, looking like Pitt, Death tells Hopkins
that his whole love speech made him want to

00:01:51.369 --> 00:01:53.299
experience an earthly life.

00:01:53.299 --> 00:01:58.700
Then the movie groans on for another two hours
and 55 minutes, mainly comprised of meaningful

00:01:58.700 --> 00:02:04.279
stares and soft-spoken dialogue, as Death
woos Forlani and bores the audience, well,

00:02:04.279 --> 00:02:05.279
to death.

00:02:05.279 --> 00:02:06.279
The Brown Bunny

00:02:06.279 --> 00:02:12.920
The 2003 Cannes Film Festival cut of Vincent
Gallo's road movie Brown Bunny was universally

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:16.530
derided as humorless, narcissistic, and boring.

00:02:16.530 --> 00:02:21.329
Roger Ebert had a special hate for it, calling
it, "The worst film in the history of the

00:02:21.329 --> 00:02:22.329
festival."

00:02:22.329 --> 00:02:26.510
The legendary critic sums it up like this:
"Imagine 90 tedious minutes of a man driving

00:02:26.510 --> 00:02:28.000
across America in a van…

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.950
Imagine a film so unendurably boring that
at one point, when he gets out of his van

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:35.040
to change his shirt, there is applause."

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.650
So, needless to say, it got a "Thumbs Down."

00:02:37.650 --> 00:02:42.159
Gallo didn't take kindly to this, and called
Ebert "a fat pig with the physique of a slave

00:02:42.159 --> 00:02:43.159
trader."

00:02:43.159 --> 00:02:47.989
Ebert responded, aping Winston Churchill,
"It is true that I am fat, but one day I will

00:02:47.989 --> 00:02:51.770
be thin, and he will still be the director
of The Brown Bunny."

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:56.530
Gallo later released an edited cut, shaving
off a quarter of the film's runtime — a

00:02:56.530 --> 00:03:01.730
version that earned a "Thumbs Up" from Ebert,
who called it "quietly, sadly, effective."

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:07.150
But the Rotten Tomatoes critical consensus
for the drama today tells another story: "More

00:03:07.150 --> 00:03:12.340
dull than hypnotic, The Brown Bunny is a pretentious
and self-indulgent bore."

00:03:12.340 --> 00:03:13.829
Dream of Light

00:03:13.829 --> 00:03:17.890
There's "watching paint dry," then there's
"watching a painting dry," which is a large

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:22.469
part of what 1992's Spanish documentary Dream
of Light is all about.

00:03:22.469 --> 00:03:27.760
To be fair, you also see painter Antonio López
García carefully set up his canvas to allow

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:31.760
him the best possible view of a nearby tree,
which is his subject.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:36.379
Aside from the actual act of painting, he
also chats with friends and has to deal with

00:03:36.379 --> 00:03:37.379
the weather.

00:03:37.379 --> 00:03:41.280
It's all very charming, if you're into this
sort of thing, but does it really need to

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:43.630
be almost a half-hour longer than Star Wars?

00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:48.300
It's the perfect pick for falling asleep on
a rainy Sunday afternoon, following a Bob

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.610
Ross marathon on PBS.

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:51.939
Manakamana

00:03:51.939 --> 00:03:57.349
In Nepal, a Hindu temple called Manakamana
sits more than 4,000 feet above sea level.

00:03:57.349 --> 00:04:03.069
Travelers of all ages and races come to the
site, from selfie-ready tourists to animal-sacrificing

00:04:03.069 --> 00:04:04.069
pilgrims.

00:04:04.069 --> 00:04:07.590
Little is known of the temple in the Western
world, but there's a documentary that sure

00:04:07.590 --> 00:04:09.969
sounds like it's about this incredible place.

00:04:09.969 --> 00:04:15.430
Except the 2014 film Manakamana doesn't show
us the temple, explain its history, or talk

00:04:15.430 --> 00:04:17.199
up its religious importance.

00:04:17.199 --> 00:04:21.819
Instead, it sits a camera in the cable car
that takes you to the temple, and shows the

00:04:21.819 --> 00:04:25.569
people riding in the car in uninterrupted
nine minute cuts.

00:04:25.569 --> 00:04:28.020
Sometimes the riders are completely silent.

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:30.120
One segment is just goats.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:34.139
Critics loved it, but agreed it may help you
drift off if you're not in the right mood,

00:04:34.139 --> 00:04:38.349
with one Vulture writer summing up the sentiment,
saying, "it’s the closest [he's] seen a

00:04:38.349 --> 00:04:41.419
film come to an act of genuine hypnosis."

00:04:41.419 --> 00:04:45.860
But when even your film's subjects seem to
fall asleep, you're probably not really exciting

00:04:45.860 --> 00:04:46.860
anyone.

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:47.860
Thanks for watching!

00:04:47.860 --> 00:04:50.940
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:50.940 --> 00:04:53.350
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

